      "shortest": true,
      "solution": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint max(int a, int b) {\n    return a > b ? a : b;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    char s[11];\n    scanf(\"%s\", s);\n    int n = strlen(s);\n\n    int best = 0;\n\n    // Try flipping each position (including no flip)\n    for (int flip = -1; flip < n; flip++) {\n\n        char temp[11];\n        strcpy(temp, s);\n\n        if (flip != -1) {\n            temp[flip] = (temp[flip] == '0') ? '1' : '0';\n        }\n\n        // Check all substrings\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            int zero = 0, one = 0;\n\n            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {\n                if (temp[j] == '0') zero++;\n                else one++;\n\n                if (zero == one) {\n                    best = max(best, j - i + 1);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", best);\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "statement": "A binary segment is called balanced if it contains the same number of {{0}}s and {{1}}s.\nYou are given a binary string [[S]]. You are allowed to flip at most one character (change {{0}} to {{1}} or {{1}} to {{0}}).\nDetermine the maximum length of a balanced contiguous segment that can be obtained.\nIf no such segment exists, output {{0}}.",
